*\n- H1 2026 orders rose 11% year on year, led by utility and data-centre customers.\n- Backlog is C$5.1B. Management says it covers 2027 revenue guidance of C$4.2B.\n- Cancellations are below 1%.\n- Q2 output was hit by an incident at the Halbrook plant. The release points to a separate restart release, which I did not find.\n\n**What that implies for 2027**\n- C$5.1B of backlog against C$4.2B of guidance is about 1.21x coverage. That leaves about C$0.9B of cushion, or roughly 18% of the backlog.\n- With cancellations under 1%, the backlog looks firm. On these disclosures, the guidance looks achievable if the company can deliver.\n- The release doesn't say how much of the backlog is scheduled for 2027. Some of it may ship in 2026 or after 2027, so the effective coverage could be lower than 1.21x.\n\n**Cross-check against recent revenue**\n- Quarterly revenue ran C$905M, C$960M, C$980M and C$890M from 2025Q3 to 2026Q2, which sums to about C$3.73B over the last four quarters.\n- Guidance of C$4.2B is about 12.6% above that. That growth is consistent with the 11% order growth.\n- The Q2 dip to C$890M, down about 9% from Q1, shows that delivery capacity is the main risk, not demand.\n\n**Main caveats**\n- Backlog is not revenue. The risks are conversion timing, Halbrook capacity and the restart, and any pricing or mix differences in the backlog.\n- This reflects only the company's own statements. I did not test them against outside sources.","ttft_ms":1323,"type":"result","duration_ms":19658,"uuid":"341f9628-dc09-4e71-b23d-261cf8e19a97","ttft_stream_ms":1197,"time_to_request_ms":74,"time_to_request_phases_ms":{"other":6,"system_prompt":8,"resume_other":0,"process_user_input":3,"input_attachments":13,"input_hooks":2,"conversation_append":1,"transcript_persist":2,"tool_pool_refresh":1,"init_message":5,"init_frame_send":2,"engine_pickup":1,"loop_entry":2,"message_prep":1,"model_config_wait":1,"autocompact":1,"request_announcements":8,"query_setup":1,"model_gates":0,"tool_